story += [P('8. How to Explain the Project','H1X'), P('30-second version','H2X'), P('“I built an Instagram analytics and machine-learning project using Python. I combined user, post, like, comment, and follow data with Pandas to create engagement features. I used a Random Forest classifier to classify users above a median engagement threshold, and two Random Forest regressors to estimate likes and comments per post from followers and posting history. I also added a confusion matrix and scatter plots for analysis. The project taught me data joining, feature engineering, model training, and responsible evaluation.”','BodyX'), P('60-90 second self-introduction version','H2X'), P('“One project I worked on is an Instagram Analytics and Influencer Prediction system. The goal was to understand social-media activity from connected CSV tables and convert it into useful predictions. I started with users as the base table and joined aggregated likes, comments, photos, and follow relationships using Pandas. I created engagement measures such as total likes, total comments, followers, posts, likes per post, and comments per post. For classification, I used a RandomForestClassifier to label users as influencer or not influencer based on an engagement threshold. For forecasting, I trained separate RandomForestRegressor models to estimate likes per post and comments per post using follower and post counts. I evaluated the classification model with accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and a confusion matrix, and created scatter plots to inspect relationships. One important lesson was that the classification label came from the same engagement features, so a perfect score should be treated as a pipeline demonstration, not a real-world performance claim. My next step would be independent labels, time-based features, and regression metrics.”','BodyX'), P('Likely questions and concise answers','H2X'), table([['Question','Answer'],['Why Random Forest?','It handles nonlinear relationships, needs little feature scaling, and can capture interactions between features. It was a sensible baseline for a small tabular dataset.'],['What is feature engineering here?','Converting raw events into user-level signals: counts of likes, comments, followers, posts, and per-post engagement rates.'],['Why left join and fillna(0)?','To retain every user, including inactive users; no recorded event means zero observed count in this dataset.'],['What does the classifier actually predict?','Whether the user’s activity-based engagement is above the dataset median, not verified commercial influence.'],['What caused 100% accuracy?','The label was derived from the same two input values, so the model effectively learned the rule used to make the label.'],['How would you evaluate regressors?','Use MAE for average absolute error, RMSE to penalize large errors, R2 for variance explained, plus a simple baseline.'],['What would you add next?','Time-based splits, independent future outcome labels, tag/content features, cross-validation, model persistence, and a dashboard.']], [5.3*cm,10.9*cm])]
